    "created_at": "2017-04-12T10:47:37Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-04-12T10:47:37Z",
    "body": "Shared memory makes workers like threads, not processes. In native world, it also doesn't make sense to kill an arbitrary thread in a multi-threaded process.\r\n\r\nIf the UA want to keep the right of killing something to save resource, it can\r\n\r\n* Kill an entire tab (usually a background one). When a user switches to that tab again, UA just reload it, so the impact on user experience is minimized. It seems some mobile browsers already have this feature. \r\n\r\n* Kill workers that explicitly allow it. E.g. add an option to the constructor:\r\n  ```js\r\n  new Worker('/path/to/js', { autokillable: true })\r\n  ```\r\n   Workers that share a memory must be all `autokillable` or not at all; they must be killed as a whole.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/html/issues/comments/293541184/reactions",
      "total_count": 0,
      "+1": 0,
      "-1": 0,
      "laugh": 0,
      "hooray": 0,
      "confused": 0,
      "heart": 0,
      "rocket": 0,
